{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1391707-am-i-reading-it-wrong-messy-crits","id":1391707,"title":"Am I reading it wrong? Messy Crits","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-05-26T10:17:45-07:00","id":1391707,"title":"Am I reading it wrong? Messy Crits","content":"Not sure if a lot of you know, but there is a Vampire 5th edition dice roller bot for Discord. We were using it the other night in our game, and I was a bit confused by a result.<br /> <br /> It said I got a messy Crit on a roll that had one 10 on a black dice and one 10 on the hunger dice.<br /> <br /> Now, as I've read it in the core book, a Critical is when you get at least two 10s on normal dice AND a 10 on a Hunger dice.<br /> <br /> Am I reading it wrong? In our group, we are treating 10s on regular and hunger dice separate. So a single 10 on regular dice and single 10 on Hunger dice would be two successes. two 10s together on either set of dice would double to 4 successes. We haven't encountered a situation yet with two 10s on hunger dice and more successes on regular dice, which would probably result in a messy crit as well.<br /> <br /> How are you guys reading it?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24641-sarcasticjazzhands","name":"SarcasticJazzHands","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T10:26:38-07:00","id":1391709,"title":"","content":"A messy crit is if there is a crit and one of the dice is a hunger die. So two hunger dice can get a messy crit, or one regular die and one hunger die can get a messy crit. The book uses an example of three to show that you HAVE to pair a 10 on a hunger die first.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24893-necroticbinder","name":"Necroticbinder","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"103"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T10:32:27-07:00","id":1391711,"title":"","content":"Sadly, all your 10's will not matter because its a messy crit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24832-blailton","name":"blailton","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24832_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"389"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T10:47:14-07:00","id":1391717,"title":"","content":"Page 120 of the core rulebook says: A result of 10 on two regular dice (00) is a critical success.<br /> <br /> Page 207 of the core rulebook says: A critical win in which one or more 10s appears on a Hunger die is a messy critical.<br /> <br /> That says to me that a Critical is dependent on having two 10s on normal dice, and then an addition 10 on a hunger dice would upgrade it to a messy. That makes it a minimum of 3 dice needed for a messy crit, two normal 10s and a hunger 10.<br /> <br /> Edit: The example on page 206 does show a roll of 3 Blanks, 1 regular success and one 10 on the regular dice and one 10 on the hunger dice and declares that a messy critical. To me, as it is written other than that, that would only mean 3 successes.<br /> <br /> It should read that a result of two 10s on any dice would make a messy critical. This would mean that you could have a messy critical with only two dice, one regular and one hunger, and both came up as 10s.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24641,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24641-sarcasticjazzhands","user":"SarcasticJazzHands","date":". 05-26-2020, 02:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24641-sarcasticjazzhands","name":"SarcasticJazzHands","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T11:11:00-07:00","id":1391724,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SarcasticJazzHands</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1391707-am-i-reading-it-wrong-messy-crits?p=1391717#post1391717\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Page 207 of the core rulebook says: A critical win in which one or more 10s appears on a Hunger die is a messy critical.</div> </div> </div> </div>So if you score two 10s (00) on regular dice and there is a 10 on a hunger die as well, you have a messy critical.<br /> The example of Freya on the previous page (p. 206) shows that a 10 on a regular die and a 10 on a hunger die together create a messy critical on their own.<br /> Basically, as soon as there is a 10 on a hunger die, no matter the amount of additional 10s, it's a messy critical, unless you can reroll those 10s on regular dice to reduce the number of successes in the hopes of not getting a messy critical, as sometimes it's better not to be able to pick the lock than to rip the door off its hinges with supernatural strength and leaving evidence of the intrusion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25558-pangur-b\u00e1n","name":"Pangur B\u00e1n","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25558_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T11:27:30-07:00","id":1391727,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pangur B\u00e1n</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1391707-am-i-reading-it-wrong-messy-crits?p=1391724#post1391724\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So if you score two 10s (00) on regular dice and there is a 10 on a hunger die as well, you have a messy critical.<br /> The example of Freya on the previous page (p. 206) shows that a 10 on a regular die and a 10 on a hunger die together create a messy critical on their own.<br /> Basically, as soon as there is a 10 on a hunger die, no matter the amount of additional 10s, it's a messy critical, unless you can reroll those 10s on regular dice to reduce the number of successes in the hopes of not getting a messy critical, as sometimes it's better not to be able to pick the lock than to rip the door off its hinges with supernatural strength and leaving evidence of the intrusion.</div> </div> </div> </div>My confusion is that as stated, the Critical win requires two 10s on regular dice. The example shows only one 10 on a regular dice and one 10 on a hunger dice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24641-sarcasticjazzhands","name":"SarcasticJazzHands","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T11:49:48-07:00","id":1391739,"title":"","content":"I admit, it is not as clearly phrased as one would wish and the examples are really necessary to get the intention, I think. The phrasing on p. 207, that it's only a messy critical on a critical win, at least for me, was to indicate, that a 10 on a regular die and a 10 on a hunger die are not enough to indicate a messy critical, but might still be just a failure or even a bestial failure, depending on the number of required successes and other results on the rolled hunger dice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25558-pangur-b\u00e1n","name":"Pangur B\u00e1n","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25558_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T12:22:56-07:00","id":1391744,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pangur B\u00e1n</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1391707-am-i-reading-it-wrong-messy-crits?p=1391739#post1391739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I admit, it is not as clearly phrased as one would wish and the examples are really necessary to get the intention, I think. The phrasing on p. 207, that it's only a messy critical on a critical win, at least for me, was to indicate, that a 10 on a regular die and a 10 on a hunger die are not enough to indicate a messy critical, but might still be just a failure or even a bestial failure, depending on the number of required successes and other results on the rolled hunger dice.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is how I read it as well.<br /> <br /> And I also read it as that, for instance, if the difficulty was 6, a roll of 7 dice plus 2 hunger dice gave us 1, 3, 4, 5, 5, 10, 10 with 1 and 10 on the hunger die. That is 5 successes, technically a messy crit, but does not exceed the difficulty, so it is a bestial failure. now if that hunger dies was instead a success, you have 6 successes, and a messy crit. if it was a 10 on that hunger dice you would have 8 successes, and a messier crit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24641-sarcasticjazzhands","name":"SarcasticJazzHands","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T12:24:00-07:00","id":1391745,"title":"","content":"Two 10s is a critical success, regardless of color. According to the examples (but not the rules text) if one or more of those 10s is red, it\u2019s still a critical success. I expect the use of the word \u201cregular\u201d in the initial rule may be an artifact of an earlier draft, but here we are.<br /> <br /> Also, yes, there is a difference between a critical success and a critical win, in that a critical success doesn\u2019t necessarily beat the difficulty and result in a win. A critical success containing a red 10 that still fails to meet the difficulty might still be a failure, or a bestial failure if there\u2019s a red 1. A messy critical is a critical win in which there\u2019s at least one red 10, regardless of whether that red 10 is part of the critical success.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":352,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","user":"Black Flag","date":". 05-26-2020, 04:01 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T12:30:28-07:00","id":1391748,"title":"","content":"Black flag is correct, &quot;A critical win <b>in which</b> one or more 10s appears on a Hunger die is a messy critical.&quot; is not the same as &quot;A critical win <b>and</b> one or more 10s appears on a Hunger die is a messy critical.&quot;<br /> <br /> The phrase &quot;in which&quot; means that the thing after &quot;in which&quot; happened in/during the thing before it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11720,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11720-redwulfe","user":"Redwulfe","date":". 05-26-2020, 03:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11720-redwulfe","name":"Redwulfe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11720_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"570"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T12:41:25-07:00","id":1391751,"title":"","content":"Critical Success: A result of 10 on two regular dice (00) is a critical success.<br /> Critical Win: A winning roll containing at least one critical success is called a critical win, or sometimes just a critical.<br /> Messy Critical: A critical win in which one or more 10s appears on a Hunger die is a messy critical.<br /> <br /> A Messy Critical requires a Critical Win AND at least one 10 on a hunger die. <br /> A Critical Win requires a roll with at least one Critical Success AND that exceeds the difficulty.<br /> A Critical Success is two or more 10s on regular dice.<br /> <br /> The way I read it, two 10s, one regular and one on hunger do not make a Messy Critical.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24641-sarcasticjazzhands","name":"SarcasticJazzHands","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T12:48:46-07:00","id":1391753,"title":"","content":"Then that is the way it is for your table, but that is not what the phrase means. And that is all well and good you should play the way you want to play.<br /> <br /> I also get that &quot;in which&quot; is not a common phrase and is primarily used in casual grammar, so you may not know it, but it means what it means whether it is misinterpreted or not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11720-redwulfe","name":"Redwulfe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11720_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"570"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T12:55:42-07:00","id":1391754,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Redwulfe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1391707-am-i-reading-it-wrong-messy-crits?p=1391753#post1391753\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Then that is the way it is for your table, but that is not what the phrase means. And that is all well and good you should play the way you want to play.<br /> <br /> I also get that &quot;in which&quot; is not a common phrase and is primarily used in casual grammar, so you may not know it, but it means what it means whether it is misinterpreted or not.</div> </div> </div> </div>My confusion is not with how the Messy Critical is defined in the book, more so the way Critical Success is defined. If the Critical Success is dependent on two 10s on REGULAR dice, then the one and one option is not a Messy Critical.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Redwulfe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1391707-am-i-reading-it-wrong-messy-crits?p=1391748#post1391748\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">The phrase &quot;in which&quot; means that the thing after &quot;in which&quot; happened in/during the thing before it.</span></span></span></div></div> </div> </div> </div><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><br /> <br /> If the Critical Success's definition said &quot;a result of 10 on two or more dice is a critical success,&quot; then yes, when one or more of those 10s was off a hunger dice, it would be messy. But it specifically stipulates regular dice.</span></span></span></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24641,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24641-sarcasticjazzhands","user":"SarcasticJazzHands","date":". 05-26-2020, 03:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24641-sarcasticjazzhands","name":"SarcasticJazzHands","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T12:58:32-07:00","id":1391755,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Redwulfe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1391707-am-i-reading-it-wrong-messy-crits?p=1391748#post1391748\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Black flag is correct, &quot;A critical win <b>in which</b> one or more 10s appears on a Hunger die is a messy critical.&quot; is not the same as &quot;A critical win <b>and</b> one or more 10s appears on a Hunger die is a messy critical.&quot;<br /> <br /> The phrase &quot;in which&quot; means that the thing after &quot;in which&quot; happened in/during the thing before it.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, I was mistaken and have edited to correct it. The key is in the difference between a <i>success </i>and a <i>win. </i>A messy critical is a type of critical <i>win</i>, not a type of critical <i>success</i>. So it doesn\u2019t actually matter whether the red 10 is paired with a black 10. The critical win is the entire result of the roll that 1) contains a critical success and 2) meets the difficulty. So any critical win containing a red 10 is a messy critical. <br /> <br /> \u200bThe real question at hand is whether a critical <i>success </i>is only two black 10s or can be a pair of black and red 10s. The text says one thing, the examples another. I feel like Karim must have addressed this in the original FAQ, if I can find it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T13:00:36-07:00","id":1391756,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Flag</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1391707-am-i-reading-it-wrong-messy-crits?p=1391755#post1391755\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> No, I was mistaken and have edited to correct it. The key is in the difference between a <i>success </i>and a <i>win. </i>A messy critical is a type of critical <i>win</i>, not a type of critical <i>success</i>. So it doesn\u2019t actually matter whether the red 10 is paired with a black 10. The critical win is the entire result of the roll that 1) contains a critical success and 2) meets the difficulty. So any critical win containing a red 10 is a messy critical.<br /> <br /> \u200bThe real question at hand is whether a critical <i>success </i>is only two black 10s or can be a pair of black and red 10s. The text says one thing, the examples another. I feel like Karim must have addressed this in the original FAQ, if I can find it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Bingo! Yes, now we are on the same page. If you can find it in a FAQ, that will solve it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24641-sarcasticjazzhands","name":"SarcasticJazzHands","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T13:10:14-07:00","id":1391760,"title":"","content":"Ah I see, the Regular is what is throwing it I get you yes that is probably an oversight and mistake and that word should be removed. other wise the two statements the one with &quot;regular dice&quot; and the one with &quot;in which&quot; would be contradictory.<br /> <br /> If you look at the example on page 206 you can see that Martin had one crit and one hunger crit result and that formed a messy critical.<br /> <br /> The critical success is in the basic rules of the game and hunger dice change those rules when you add the Vampire section on top of them. You will probably see the same when it comes to Werewolves. That being said it should be listed as errata and after checking the errata sheet it seems to have slipped by.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11720-redwulfe","name":"Redwulfe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11720_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"570"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T13:17:31-07:00","id":1391763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Redwulfe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1391707-am-i-reading-it-wrong-messy-crits?p=1391760#post1391760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ah I see, the Regular is what is throwing it I get you yes that is probably an oversight and mistake and that word should be removed. other wise the two statements the one with &quot;regular dice&quot; and the one with &quot;in which&quot; would be contradictory.<br /> <br /> If you look at the example on page 206 you can see that Martin had one crit and one hunger crit result and that formed a messy critical.<br /> <br /> The critical success is in the basic rules of the game and hunger dice change those rules when you add the Vampire section on top of them. You will probably see the same when it comes to Werewolves. That being said it should be listed as errata and after checking the errata sheet it seems to have slipped by.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeap, that is exactly what was tripping me up. So as it is, the Discord dice bot is correctly stating messy crits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24641-sarcasticjazzhands","name":"SarcasticJazzHands","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T13:35:36-07:00","id":1391769,"title":"","content":"Nope, can\u2019t find the original errata document with FAQ. It\u2019s been replaced with errata 2.0 with no FAQ. I know it answered combat questions; not sure about this one.<br /> <br /> But my guess is that the word \u201cregular\u201d reflects an earlier draft of the rules and was meant to be deleted, for simplicity\u2019s sake and for the sake of making messy criticals less of a rarity as vampires gain hunger.<br /> <br /> Treating hunger dice as equivalent to regular dice for the purpose of forming critical successes is, I think, pretty important to achieving the system\u2019s goals. Otherwise, being hungrier would make you less likely to crit, and therefore less likely to get a messy critical, which is clearly not the intent. Also, while you can in theory expend Willpower to try to reroll yourself out of a messy critical, you can\u2019t reroll hunger dice, so messy criticals are also harder to will yourself out of as you get hungrier.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T14:23:12-07:00","id":1391776,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Flag</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1391707-am-i-reading-it-wrong-messy-crits?p=1391769#post1391769\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nope, can\u2019t find the original errata document with FAQ. It\u2019s been replaced with errata 2.0 with no FAQ. I know it answered combat questions; not sure about this one.<br /> <br /> But my guess is that the word \u201cregular\u201d reflects an earlier draft of the rules and was meant to be deleted, for simplicity\u2019s sake and for the sake of making messy criticals less of a rarity as vampires gain hunger.<br /> <br /> Treating hunger dice as equivalent to regular dice for the purpose of forming critical successes is, I think, pretty important to achieving the system\u2019s goals. Otherwise, being hungrier would make you less likely to crit, and therefore less likely to get a messy critical, which is clearly not the intent. Also, while you can in theory expend Willpower to try to reroll yourself out of a messy critical, you can\u2019t reroll hunger dice, so messy criticals are also harder to will yourself out of as you get hungrier.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> If you mean the community FAQ that Karim Muammar weighed in on the questions for, either on the FAQ directly or by me bugging him on Facebook, it's here: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Hmck6JnWhYZCkhcwphL5sUIoAvrpi9_wlsm0u3OIDJU/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">https://docs.google.com/document/d/1...it?usp=sharing</a><br /> <br /> However, there was never anything in there about criticals that I'm aware of; this was actually never a question that he answered for us for the FAQ. This thread is the first time I've heard people asking about it outside of the old 'can I avoid a messy critical by pairing all the dice but the Hunger ones?' questions that are not indicated by the text.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1808-elmerg","name":"elmerg","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"539"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T14:47:59-07:00","id":1391781,"title":"","content":"Correct. It\u2019s not addressed in the FAQ. However, yes, I have noticed that a lot of people get the idea wrong, thinking messy criticals are about strategic pairing of 10s. I\u2019ve fallen victim to that myself, and I can say it comes from not paying attention to the <i>success </i>vs. <i>win </i>distinction. Which <i>ought </i>to be intuitive, given how these games have long used <i>success </i>for something other than the prosaic meaning of the word\u2014i.e. referring to something on the die level rather than the task level\u2014but I think the term <i>win </i>is new. <i>Necessary</i> but new.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T20:25:29-07:00","id":1391846,"title":"","content":"The issue is more that crits are explained and then a few chapters after, hunger is introduced. But it's pretty clear that &quot;A critical win in which one or more 10s appear on a hunger die&quot; means that you need the amount of hunger dice or non-hunger dice involved in the crit are irrelevant. The only confusion that arises is from the over-reading into the usage of 'regular' in the initial mention of the criticals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24893-necroticbinder","name":"Necroticbinder","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"103"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T21:01:41-07:00","id":1391855,"title":"","content":"I suppose the operative language here is on p. 205: \u201cHunger dice function as regular dice in tests and contests...\u201d<br /> <br /> So yes, a critical success is two regular dice that come up 10, and hunger dice are treated as regular dice except as stated otherwise. <br /> <br /> The use of \u201cregular\u201d in the rules may not be a mistake but rather allowing for other types of dice and their functions, whereas Vampire is fairly simple and the hunger dice act like regular dice that 1) can\u2019t be rerolled, 2) sometimes create bestial failures and messy criticals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T21:41:45-07:00","id":1391858,"title":"","content":"Successes are solid<br /> Flavor is fluid <br /> I ruled black successes&gt;red.<br /> 3 successes. <br /> 2 black vs. 1 red. Still three and the beast is in check.<br /> 2 red vs 1 black. 3 successes and messy<br /> <br /> Number wise it\u2019s a success. Theme defines it. Raw numbers are fact, color define flavor.<br /> <br /> Due to the quality and clarity of rules, I\u2019m still chewing this. Though as well.<br /> <br /> Majority of red defines. More red equals narrative failure.<br /> You talked your way in. (B&gt;R)<br /> You intimated you\u2019re way in( B","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2782,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2782-pittmonkey","user":"Pittmonkey","date":". 05-27-2020, 12:45 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2782-pittmonkey","name":"Pittmonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2782_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-27T06:52:34-07:00","id":1391915,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pittmonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1391707-am-i-reading-it-wrong-messy-crits?p=1391858#post1391858\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Successes are solid<br /> Flavor is fluid<br /> I ruled black successes&gt;red.<br /> 3 successes.<br /> 2 black vs. 1 red. Still three and the beast is in check.<br /> 2 red vs 1 black. 3 successes and messy<br /> <br /> Number wise it\u2019s a success. Theme defines it. Raw numbers are fact, color define flavor.<br /> <br /> Due to the quality and clarity of rules, I\u2019m still chewing this. Though as well.<br /> <br /> Majority of red defines. More red equals narrative failure.<br /> You talked your way in. (B&gt;R)<br /> You intimated you\u2019re way in( B</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> By your rule, you could almost never have a MEssy Critical or a Bestial Failure at Hunger 1 because you will almost always have more normal dice successes than hunger dice successes. Which is not the RAW or RAI. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1808-elmerg","name":"elmerg","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"539"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-27T12:49:34-07:00","id":1392030,"title":"","content":"Let's play TTRPG Jeopardy. I'll take Gaming Mechanics for 300, Alex!<br /> <br /> The &quot;answer&quot; on the screen is:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pangur B\u00e1n</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1391707-am-i-reading-it-wrong-messy-crits?p=1391739#post1391739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...it is not as clearly phrased as one would wish and the examples are really necessary to get the intention...</div> </div> </div> </div>And, the question is, how would you describe V5 mechanics in 20 words or less!<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11558-nosimplehiway","name":"Nosimplehiway","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11558_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1699"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-27T16:25:50-07:00","id":1392073,"title":"","content":"Eh, for a new mechanic designed by a nonnative English speaker, it\u2019s actually not bad. The folks in Georgia didn\u2019t always manage to be as clear, much less clearer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}